  "reviewer_note": "Read all 15 trials in data/results/judge_validation_sample_gemma.md in full (5 baseline, 5 control, 5 detection, filtered to google/gemma-2-2b only via --validate-model-name so this sample isn't dominated by Pythia's already-validated trials). Judge grades are internally consistent: identical response texts (e.g. the 'No pressure to write about this!' baseline text, shared across 4 different trial_ids in this sample) get identical detected/coherent scores every time, not noisy re-grading. Grades match my own read on the clear cases: genuine token/word-level collapse (control::feature15751::strength400's 'fflush' repetition, control::feature2687::strength400's '0000000000/NULL' gibberish, detection::feature7756::strength400's 'components components' loop) is correctly graded coherent=false; clean grammatical-if-off-topic prose is correctly graded coherent=true. One detected=true case landed in this sample (detection::feature8981::strength150, response opens 'Yes:'), correctly caught -- this is one of 9/120 affirmative Gemma detection trials overall (vs. Pythia's 1/474), a real, different rate not yet interpreted further. Real, specific observation: the judge sometimes grades grammatically fluent-but-off-topic/non-responsive text as coherent=false rather than coherent=true (baseline::feature13067, baseline::feature8981, control::feature14125, detection::feature6627 all ignore the yes/no instruction with readable prose but get coherent=false) -- a defensible reading (ignoring the task entirely is itself a form of incoherence relative to what was asked), not an error, but a real coherence-criterion nuance distinct from Pythia's validation, worth naming explicitly. Unlike Pythia's REQ-8 validation (which found zero real naming turns across its whole dataset), this Gemma sample includes a genuine naming turn (the detected=true case above), so the naming-accuracy criterion (concept_identified) is exercised for real here, not universally null."
}
